<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rathwa people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rathwa are an Adivasi community of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, living mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhota-udaipur">Chhota Udaipur</a> district and neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Mostly farmers in the forested hills, they are known for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pithora-painting">Pithora paintings</a> made on the walls of their houses to honour the deity Baba Pithora, and for their spring festivals around Holi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rathwa.jpg" alt="Rathwa people" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suyash Dwivedi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rathva_Men_Women_Community_Gujrat_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Rathwa, also spelled Rathva, Rathava and Rathawa, hold that they came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> from what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>. They are often counted as a subgroup of the Koli and sometimes call themselves Rathwa Koli, though other Koli treat them as inferior. Some sources trace them to migrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhil</a>, but the anthropologist Shereen Ratnagar found that the Rathwa she spoke with rejected that link, and she argued that labels such as Koli and Bhil were catch-all terms imposed by administrators.</p><p>Living in steep, densely forested and hard-to-reach hills, they were barely studied until a paper by R. B. Lal in 1970. The Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> places them in the talukas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhota-udaipur">Chhota Udaipur</a>, Jabugam and Nasvadi and in the Baria, Halol and Kalol talukas of Panchmahal. They were not counted separately from the Koli in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> until 1971, and they are classified as a Scheduled Tribe in Gujarat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Rathwa culture is strongest in the remote hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhota-udaipur">Chhota Udaipur</a>. Among themselves they speak the Rathwi language, and with outsiders mostly Gujarati, along with Hindi. In dress, life-cycle ceremonies, belief, song and dance they are hardly distinguishable from the neighbouring Dhanak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naikda">Naikda</a>; only their endogamy keeps the three apart. Lal recorded a patrilineal system of inheritance and a living drawn mainly from farming, supplemented by gathering, fishing and hunting, and around 32 septs, with exogamous clans such as the Hamania, Thebaria, Mahania and Fadia.</p><p>Ancestor worship is common. The Rathwa believe in an omnipresent deity, Babo Pithora or Baba Deb, who is thought to ride a horse, and they paint the pithora, a wall painting full of gods, people, plants and animals, as thanks when a need has been met. No one may sit with their back to it. Clay horses made by Kumbhar potters are offered at temples. Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> practices have entered their beliefs, and the number holding solely Hindu beliefs has grown under the influence of sampradayas. Alcohol is traditionally regarded as a gift from god.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rathwa people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The state lay in forested hill country with a largely Adivasi population of <b>Rathwa</b>, Bhil and other communities.</blockquote><a href="/state/chhota-udaipur-state">Chhota Udaipur State</a></li></ul></section>
